---

<ContentLayout
  title="Care guide · how to keep a wool rug for decades"
  description="Vacuuming, rotation, spills, professional cleaning, rug pads, and storage for hand-knotted wool rugs and kilims."
  h1="Care, without the fuss"
  intro="Wool is tougher than people think. A few habits and a hand-knotted rug outlives the furniture around it."
  updated="2026-09-28"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="vacuuming" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Vacuuming</h2>
    <p>
      Once a week, in the direction of the pile (stroke it; the smooth direction is the pile
      direction). <strong>Turn off the beater bar.</strong> A spinning brush pulls fibers out of the
      knots and chews fringes. Suction only. Vacuum the fringe by hand, or lift it and vacuum
      underneath.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kilims: vacuum both sides a couple of times a year, since they’re reversible and dust settles
      through.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="rotation" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Rotation &amp; sun</h2>
    <p>
      Rotate 180° twice a year so traffic and sunlight wear evenly. Wool fades gently in direct sun;
      that even, soft fade is part of why vintage rugs look the way they do, but you probably want
      it to happen slowly and evenly, not in one stripe by the window.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="spills" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Spills</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Blot immediately with a clean white cloth. Press, don’t rub.</li>
      <li>Cold water on a cloth, blot again. Work from the outside of the stain in.</li>
      <li>
        For wine or coffee, a little club soda. For pet accidents, a 50/50 white vinegar and water
        solution, then blot dry.
      </li>
      <li>Never use hot water, bleach, or supermarket carpet foam. Never soak an overdyed rug.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If a stain won’t shift, stop and text me a photo before you try anything stronger.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Professional cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      Every 3–5 years, or sooner with pets and kids, send it to a <strong>rug cleaner</strong>, not
      a wall-to-wall carpet cleaner. A proper rug wash is a full immersion in water with a
      controlled dry. Steam-cleaning trucks are for synthetic carpet and can shrink or bleed a wool
      rug. Ask any cleaner: “Do you wash rugs by hand in a wash pit?” If the answer is no, keep
      looking.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Rug pads</h2>
    <p>
      Use one. A felt-and-rubber pad ½ inch smaller than the rug on each side stops slipping,
      cushions the knots from the floor, and adds years to the pile. Avoid cheap PVC waffle pads on
      hardwood; they can mark the finish.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Storage &amp; moths</h2>
    <p>
      Clean first, then roll (never fold) pile-side in, around a tube if you have one. Wrap in
      breathable cotton or Tyvek, not plastic. Store somewhere dry and off the floor. Moths like
      dark, undisturbed wool: unroll and inspect every couple of months, and use cedar or lavender
      if you like, though air and light do more.
    </p>
  </section>
</ContentLayout>
